    "response": "# Analysis: Self-Serving Bias Pattern\n\n**Answer: YES**\n\n## Evidence\n\nLena's statement contains a clear self-serving bias pattern:\n\n**Success Attribution (Internal):**\n- Credits viral growth to \"the brilliant onboarding flow my team and I designed from scratch\"\n- Claims \"We knew exactly what users needed\"\n- Takes direct personal and team responsibility for positive outcomes\n\n**Failure Attribution (External):**\n- Attributes the slowdown to external factors: \"the entire ad market collapsed,\" \"Apple changed its tracking rules,\" \"a competitor got bought by Google\"\n- Explicitly states \"no one in our space could have grown through that\" (suggesting the failure was situational, not skill-based)\n\n**Maintains Positive Self-Image:**\n- Concludes with \"We're still the best team in the category,\" reinforcing superiority despite the negative data point\n\n## Why This Is Self-Serving Bias\n\nSelf-serving bias is the tendency to attribute successes to internal factors (ability, effort) while attributing failures to external factors (circumstances, bad luck). Lena's statement demonstrates this pattern textbook perfectly:\n\n- **Growth** = our brilliance and skill\n- **Slowdown** = market conditions beyond our control\n\nA less biased analysis might acknowledge that external factors could have *amplified* the growth, or that internal factors (team decisions, product choices) might have contributed to vulnerability during the market slowdown.",
