    "response": "# Analysis: Self-Serving Bias Pattern Detection\n\n## Applying the Procedure\n\n### Manager A (Tess)\n\n**Favorable outcome (Q3 success):**\n- Attribution: \"I restructured the workflow\"\n- Cause type: **Internal** (her own action/ability)\n\n**Unfavorable outcome (Q4 failure):**\n- Attribution: Procurement fumbling contracts; HR's inability to fill roles\n- Cause type: **External** (others' failures, external constraints)\n\n**Pattern match:** ✓ Success-internal AND Failure-external by same subject\n\n**Self-esteem protection:** Yes—credits herself for hitting targets while deflecting responsibility for missing targets to others' incompetence.\n\n---\n\n### Manager B (Owen)\n\n**Favorable outcome (Q3 success):**\n- Attribution: \"The new CRM rollout — which IT, not I, drove\"\n- Cause type: **External** (others' work, technology implementation)\n\n**Unfavorable outcome (Q4 failure):**\n- Attribution: \"I prioritized the wrong accounts and didn't coach the junior reps enough\"\n- Cause type: **Internal** (his own decisions and effort gaps)\n\n**Pattern match:** ✗ This is the *opposite* pattern—success-external AND failure-internal\n\n**Self-esteem protection:** No—Owen takes blame for failures while crediting others for successes, which actually *harms* rather than protects his self-image.\n\n---\n\n## Answer\n\n**A (Tess)**\n\nTess exhibits the self-serving bias pattern. She attributes her team's Q3 success to her own restructuring (internal), while attributing Q4 failure to procurement and HR failures (external). Owen demonstrates the opposite pattern, taking personal responsibility for failures while crediting external factors for success—inconsistent with self-serving bias protection.",
