    "skill_name": "accident-fallacy",
    "mode": "singlecall",
    "response": "# Answer: No\n\nThis argument does **not** commit the fallacy of accident.\n\n## Analysis\n\n**1. Identifying the General Rule**\nThe major premise is \"The interior angles of any Euclidean triangle sum to 180 degrees.\"\n\n**2. Identifying the Specific Case**\nTriangle ABC is the specific instance being evaluated—a Euclidean triangle drawn on flat paper with straight line segments.\n\n**3. Checking for a Recognized Exception**\nThis is the critical step. The major premise is **not a rule of thumb or generalization with typical exceptions**—it is a **universally true mathematical principle**. In Euclidean geometry, the 180-degree angle sum is a necessary, exceptionless theorem. There are no recognized, legitimate exceptions to this rule within the Euclidean system.\n\n**4. Application to the Specific Case**\nTriangle ABC meets all the conditions of the rule without qualification. It is explicitly established as a Euclidean triangle, so no exception applies.\n\n**5. Argument Form**\nWhile the argument does move from a general statement to a specific case (classic deductive form), it is not a statistical syllogism based on a rule of thumb. It is a **valid deductive argument with a universally true premise**.\n\n## Why Not the Fallacy of Accident\n\nThe fallacy of accident requires that:\n- A generalization with recognized exceptions be applied to a case\n- That case falls under a legitimate exception\n- The exception is ignored\n\nNone of these conditions are met here. The major premise has no exceptions to overlook, so the critic's objection is unfounded.",
