    "response": "# Answer: **No**\n\n## Analysis\n\nThe student is incorrect. This argument does not commit the fallacy of accident.\n\n### Why This Argument Is Valid\n\nThe argument has the following structure:\n1. All bachelors are unmarried men (universal definition)\n2. Tomas is a bachelor (established fact)\n3. Therefore, Tomas is an unmarried man (logical conclusion)\n\nThis is a valid categorical syllogism. The reasoning properly applies a universal statement to a particular case—exactly what deductive logic is supposed to do.\n\n### Why This Is Not the Fallacy of Accident\n\nThe fallacy of accident occurs when a general principle is applied to a particular case where a specific exception or unusual circumstance actually applies. However:\n\n- The student's reasoning assumes there could be an \"unstated exception\" to the definition of bachelor\n- But \"bachelor\" is definitionally equivalent to \"unmarried man\"—this is not a contingent generalization subject to exceptions\n- There is no possible case where someone could be both a bachelor AND married, because being unmarried is part of what makes someone a bachelor\n\nThe student confuses applying a general rule to a particular case (which is valid deduction) with wrongly ignoring relevant exceptions to a rule (which is the actual fallacy of accident).\n\n### Conclusion\n\nThe argument commits no fallacy. It is a straightforward, valid deductive inference from a definition and a factual premise.",
