    "response": "**No.**\n\nThe student is incorrect. This argument does not commit the fallacy of accident.\n\n**Reasoning:**\n\n1. **The major premise is universally true, not a generalization.** The statement \"Every bachelor is an unmarried man\" is not a rule of thumb or statistical generalization—it is a definitional, exceptionless claim. Being unmarried is constitutive of what it means to be a bachelor. There are no legitimate exceptions to this rule.\n\n2. **No recognized exception exists.** The student suggests Tomas \"might fall under some unstated exception,\" but this is purely speculative. There is no plausible, recognized exception to the definition of \"bachelor\" that would apply here. A person cannot be a bachelor *and* married simultaneously—that would be a logical contradiction, not an exception.\n\n3. **The fallacy of accident requires both a generalization AND an ignored exception.** Per the constraints, both elements must be present. Here, there is an exceptionless definition but no identifiable exception to overlook.\n\n4. **This is valid deductive reasoning.** The argument follows the form of a categorical syllogism with a universal premise, and it validly reaches its conclusion.\n\nThe student has confused a rule-based argument with a generalization-based one. The fallacy of accident applies to sweeping generalizations (like \"taking property is theft\") applied to cases that fall under recognized exceptions (like lawful tax collection). No such exception applies to the definition of \"bachelor.\"",
