    "response": "# Analysis\n\n**Yes.**\n\n## Justification\n\nThe reporter's question contains the presupposition that **the office has not yet disciplined anyone** for the leak.\n\n### Applying the Procedure:\n\n1. **Interrogative sentence identified**: \"Given the leak, why hasn't the office disciplined anyone yet?\"\n\n2. **Presupposition extracted**: For this question to be coherent, it must assume that:\n   - Disciplinary action has not occurred (the negative fact \"hasn't disciplined\")\n   - This is despite the leak being a matter that could warrant discipline\n\n3. **Logical entailment test**: Any answer Lin provides—whether explaining why discipline hasn't happened, announcing that it will happen, or defending the lack of action—logically requires accepting that no discipline has yet occurred. The respondent cannot answer the \"why\" without first conceding the premise.\n\n4. **Is it contested or unestablished?**: Yes. Lin's opening statement mentions only that a leak occurred and was confirmed. Lin makes **no claim** about whether discipline has or hasn't been imposed. The reporter's assertion of non-action is entirely unestablished at this point.\n\n5. **Non-trivial and unconceded?**: Yes. This is a substantive claim about institutional action (or inaction) that Lin never granted.\n\n## Conclusion\n\nThe reporter smuggles in the unconceded presupposition that disciplinary measures have not been taken, forcing Lin to defend or explain that absence rather than first establishing whether it's even true.",
