    "response": "# Analysis\n\nThe proposition smuggled into the attorney's question is:\n\n**\"The defendant paid you to keep quiet.\"**\n\n## Explanation\n\nThis is a classic example of a **complex question** (also called a \"loaded question\" or *argumentum ad hominem*). The attorney's question presupposes as true a fact that has not been established—namely, that payment occurred—and then asks the witness to merely specify the amount.\n\nThe question has the logical structure: \"How much X happened?\" which assumes X happened as a precondition of answering.\n\nAs the passage explicitly states, \"The trial record contains no testimony, exhibit, or stipulation indicating that any payment passed between the defendant and Mr. Patel.\" The attorney has introduced this completely unsupported claim within a question, forcing the witness to either:\n1. Accept the false premise by answering \"how much,\" or\n2. Explicitly deny the premise to correct the record\n\nThis violates fundamental rules of evidence in most jurisdictions, as it attempts to introduce inflammatory information disguised as a question rather than through proper testimony.",
