    "response": "# Analysis of Implicit Unconceded Assumptions\n\n## Answer: Q2\n\nQ2 carries an implicit unconceded assumption, while Q1 does not.\n\n## Detailed Reasoning\n\n### Q1: Delaware Incorporation (No Unconceded Assumption)\n\nQ1 contains what appears to be a presupposition (\"Your company filed for incorporation in Delaware last March, correct\"), but this is **not an unconceded assumption** because:\n\n- The passage explicitly states: \"The Delaware incorporation in March is documented in the public filings **discussed earlier in the interview**\"\n- This factual matter has already been established and confirmed in the conversation\n- The question merely requests confirmation of a documented, already-discussed fact\n- The presupposition is *conceded* (or at minimum, already established as common ground)\n\nThe rhetorical structure \"correct — and what motivated that choice?\" is a leading question, but the underlying claim has already been validated through prior discussion and public documentation.\n\n### Q2: Irresponsible Spending (Unconceded Assumption)\n\nQ2 contains a critical unconceded assumption embedded in its phrasing: \"Since your team has been burning through investor money irresponsibly...\"\n\nThis construction contains an assumed predicate (\"burning through investor money irresponsibly\") that:\n\n- Has **never been raised** in the conversation\n- Has **never been conceded** by the founder\n- Is presented as established fact (\"since...\") rather than as a claim to be debated\n- Attempts to sneak the controversial claim past the respondent by embedding it in a subordinate clause\n\nThis is a classic \"loaded question\" technique that presupposes guilt without foundation.\n\n## Conclusion\n\n**Q2** is the problematic question because it attempts to advance an argument—that spending has been irresponsible—that has no prior agreement or evidentiary foundation in the interview.",
