    "response": "# Analysis\n\nThe trainee's question smuggles in the proposition: **\"You are alcohol dependent\"**\n\n## Why This Is Problematic\n\nThe question uses a presupposition—an assumption embedded in the structure of the question itself that the client is invited to accept without examination. Specifically:\n\n- **The explicit question asks:** \"How long have you been hiding this?\"\n- **The implicit assumption:** That the client IS dependent and HAS been hiding it\n\n## Evidence from the Passage\n\nThe passage explicitly notes that:\n1. The client described only a **factual pattern** (\"two glasses most evenings\")\n2. The client made **no mention of dependence**\n3. **No diagnostic assessment** exists in the file\n\n## The Clinical Problem\n\nBy phrasing the question this way, the trainee:\n- Converts undiagnosed drinking behavior into presumed pathology\n- Pressures the client to either confirm a false premise or spend effort denying it\n- Bypasses proper diagnostic procedure\n- Potentially suggests the trainee is imposing their own interpretation rather than gathering information\n\nThis is a classic example of a **loaded question** that contaminates the clinical assessment process.",
