    "response": "# Analysis\n\n## Interrogative Sentence Identified\n\"How long have you been hiding your alcohol dependence from your family?\"\n\n## Presuppositions (propositions that must be true for the question to make sense)\n1. The client has alcohol dependence\n2. The client is hiding this dependence from their family\n3. There is a temporal duration to this hiding behavior\n\n## Testing Against the Passage\n\n**Presupposition 1: The client has alcohol dependence**\n- The passage explicitly states: \"The client had not described dependence, only the two-glass evening pattern\"\n- No diagnostic assessment for alcohol use disorder appears in the file\n- A direct answer (e.g., \"For three years\") logically entails acceptance that the client *does* have alcohol dependence\n- This presupposition is **not established as fact** in the passage—it is contested/undiagnosed\n\n**Presupposition 2 & 3:** These depend on Presupposition 1 being true first.\n\n## Conclusion\n\nThe proposition smuggled past the client is:\n\n**\"You have alcohol dependence\"**\n\n(Alternatively phrased: The client has an alcohol use disorder/is alcohol-dependent)\n\nThis is a non-trivial presupposition that the client never conceded and for which no clinical evidence has been documented. Answering the question would force the client to accept a diagnostic claim that was never established.",
