    "response": "# Analysis\n\n## Interrogative Identification\nThe loaded question is: \"So when are you going to stop sneaking food behind my back?\"\n\n## Presupposition Mapping\nFor this question to be coherent, the following propositions must already be true:\n1. Sam sneaks food behind the parent's back\n2. This is an ongoing or habitual behavior\n3. Sam will continue this behavior into the future (hence \"when\" will you *stop*)\n\n## Testing Against Constraints\n\n**Presupposition 1 & 2 (Habitual sneaking):** \n- Sam openly admitted to eating the cookies and apologized—no concealment occurred\n- No other pattern of sneaking has been \"mentioned, observed, or admitted\"\n- A direct answer to \"when will you stop\" would logically require Sam to concede that sneaking is already an established pattern\n- This presupposition is **not established fact** in the passage; it contradicts Sam's actual behavior\n\n**Does answering force concession?**\nYes. Replying \"I'll stop next week\" or \"I never will\" both accept the premise that sneaking is already happening.\n\n## Verdict\nThis question clears all criteria for a loaded question with an implicit assumption. The surface question asks about timing; the smuggled proposition is dispositional/habitual.\n\n---\n\n## Answer\n\n**Sam has a pattern of sneaking food** (or: **Sam is a habitual food-sneaker**)",
