    "response": "# Analysis\n\n## Step 1: Identify Physical Objects and Spatial Relations\n\n**Objects:**\n- Wooden crate\n- Truck\n- Front doorway\n- Foyer/hallway\n- Interior office door\n\n**Stated spatial relations:**\n1. Crate slid through front doorway with ~1 inch clearance on each side\n2. Crate would not pass through office door; side scraped both jambs\n3. Crate was not rotated between attempts\n4. Front doorway width: 36 inches\n5. Office door width: 30 inches\n6. Hallway width: 48 inches\n\n## Step 2: Derive Constraints from Spatial Relations\n\n**From the front doorway passage:**\n- \"About an inch of clearance on each side\" means the crate fit through with minimal margin\n- Clearance on each side ≈ 1 inch\n- If doorway = 36 inches and clearance = ~1 inch per side, then:\n  - **Crate width ≈ 36 − 2(1) = approximately 34 inches** (or slightly less)\n\n**From the office door failure:**\n- \"Crate's side scraped both jambs\" means the crate's width exceeded the doorway width\n- Office door = 30 inches\n- The crate could not fit through\n- **Crate width > 30 inches**\n\n**Orientation constraint:**\n- Crate was not rotated between attempts, so the same dimension (width) was presented to both doorways\n\n## Step 3: Check Consistency\n\nBoth constraints are consistent:\n- Crate width ≈ 34 inches (derived from front doorway relation)\n- Crate width > 30 inches (derived from office door relation)\n- 34 > 30 ✓\n\n## Answer\n\n**Yes**\n\nThe crate's width is greater than 30 inches. This is directly established by the fact that the crate scraped both jambs of the 30-inch office door, meaning its width exceeded 30 inches. Additionally, the front doorway passage (36 inches with ~1 inch clearance on each side) suggests the crate's width is approximately 34 inches, which further confirms it exceeds 30 inches.",
