    "response": "# Solution: Height of One Box\n\n## Analysis\n\nTo find the height of one box, I need to use the constraint that the boxes are stacked directly on top of each other, with the top of the green box flush with the shelf.\n\n## Key Information from the Passage\n\n1. **Stack arrangement**: Red box (bottom) → Blue box (middle) → Green box (top)\n2. **Constraint**: The top of the green box is flush with the shelf\n3. **Shelf height**: 38 inches above the floor\n4. **Box heights**: All three boxes are the same height\n\n## Logical Deduction\n\nSince all three boxes have identical heights and are stacked directly on top of one another:\n\n- The red box sits on the floor, so its top is at height = **h** (where h = height of one box)\n- The blue box rests on the red box, so its top is at height = **2h**\n- The green box rests on the blue box, so its top is at height = **3h**\n\nThe top of the green box is flush with the shelf at 38 inches:\n\n**3h = 38 inches**\n\n**h = 38 ÷ 3 = 12⅔ inches (or 12.67 inches)**\n\n## Answer\n\n**Each box is 12⅔ inches (approximately 12.67 inches) tall.**\n\nThis can also be expressed as **38/3 inches** or approximately **12 feet 8 inches** if we need whole measurements.",
