    "response": "# Analysis of the Ladder Length Problem\n\n## Problem Setup\n\nThe passage describes a right triangle formed by:\n- **Vertical leg**: The height of the hayloft opening = 12 feet\n- **Horizontal leg**: The distance from the barn wall to the ladder's base = 5 feet\n- **Hypotenuse**: The ladder itself (unknown length)\n\n## Solution\n\nUsing the **Pythagorean theorem**: a² + b² = c²\n\nWhere:\n- a = 5 feet (horizontal distance)\n- b = 12 feet (vertical height)\n- c = length of ladder (what we're solving for)\n\n**Calculation:**\n- 5² + 12² = c²\n- 25 + 144 = c²\n- 169 = c²\n- c = √169\n- **c = 13 feet**\n\n## Answer\n\n**The ladder is 13 feet long.**\n\n## Note on Irrelevant Details\n\nThe passage includes two pieces of information that don't affect the answer:\n1. The bale of hay near the ladder's base\n2. The rooster perched 20 feet away on a fence\n\nThese are extraneous details that test whether the reader can identify the essential geometric information needed to solve the problem. The only relevant facts are the 5-foot horizontal distance and the 12-foot vertical height, which form a classic 5-12-13 Pythagorean triple.",
