    "skill_name": "spatial-reasoning",
    "mode": "singlecall",
    "response": "# Analysis: Spatial Reasoning Challenge\n\n## Objects and Spatial Relations\n\n**Physical objects identified:**\n- Ladder (straight, leaning)\n- Barn wall (vertical)\n- Ground (level)\n- Hayloft opening (at a specific height)\n- Bale of hay (position reference)\n- Rooster and fence (position reference)\n\n**Key spatial relations:**\n1. Ladder leans against the barn wall\n2. Ladder's top reaches the hayloft opening's lower edge\n3. Hayloft opening's lower edge is 12 feet above ground\n4. Ladder's base rests 5 feet from the barn wall\n5. Ground is level\n\n## Derived Constraints\n\nFrom these relations, I can infer:\n\n- The ladder forms the **hypotenuse** of a right triangle\n- One leg: **vertical distance** = 12 feet (height to hayloft opening)\n- Other leg: **horizontal distance** = 5 feet (base distance from wall)\n- The ladder's top endpoint and base endpoint establish these constraints\n\nThe bale of hay and rooster provide contextual detail but do not constrain the ladder's length.\n\n## Solution\n\nUsing the Pythagorean theorem:\n\n**c² = a² + b²**\n\n**c² = 12² + 5²**\n\n**c² = 144 + 25**\n\n**c² = 169**\n\n**c = 13 feet**\n\n## Answer\n\n**The ladder is 13 feet long.**\n\nThis is a classic 5-12-13 right triangle, a Pythagorean triple. The spatial constraint that the ladder reaches from a point 5 feet from the wall to a point 12 feet above ground, with the ladder remaining straight and in contact with level ground and the barn wall, necessitates this length.",
