    "response": "# Analysis: Determining Plot C's Direction from the Toolshed\n\n## Setting Up the Spatial Framework\n\nLet me establish a coordinate system to work through this systematically.\n\n**Given Information:**\n- Three plots (A, B, C) arranged west to east in a row\n- Footpath runs north of all plots (along river)\n- Toolshed is south of Plot B\n- From Plot A: toolshed is to the southeast\n- From Plot C: gardener walks west to reach shed, then south\n\n## Working Through the Constraints\n\n### Constraint 1: Toolshed Position Relative to Plot A\nThe toolshed being **southeast** of Plot A means:\n- The shed is south AND east of Plot A\n- Plot A is northwest of the shed\n\n### Constraint 2: Toolshed Position Relative to Plot C\nThe path from Plot C is: **west → toolshed → south**\n\nThis is critical. If the gardener walks west first and then south:\n- The toolshed must be **west** of Plot C (requiring the westward leg)\n- The toolshed must be **south** of Plot C (requiring the southern leg)\n- Therefore, the toolshed is **southwest** of Plot C\n\n### Verification of Internal Consistency\n- Plot A < Plot B < Plot C (west to east arrangement)\n- Toolshed is southeast of A: ✓ (shed is east of A)\n- Toolshed is southwest of C: ✓ (shed is west of C)\n- Toolshed is south of B: ✓ (consistent with being between A and C horizontally while being south)\n\nThis configuration is geometrically consistent.\n\n## Answer\n\n**Plot C lies to the NORTHEAST of the toolshed.**\n\nThis is the reciprocal direction: if the toolshed is southwest of Plot C, then Plot C must be northeast of the toolshed.",
