    "response": "# Analysis\n\n**Answer: No**\n\n## Reasoning\n\nThe challenge asks whether we can conclude a floor-3 smoke detector triggered based solely on two things:\n1. The building protocol (if floor-3 smoke detector triggers → stairwell doors unlock)\n2. The door unlock event (stairwell doors unlocked at 2:14 p.m.)\n\nThis is a logical fallacy problem. The protocol establishes:\n- **If** smoke detector triggers → **Then** doors unlock\n\nHowever, observing that the doors unlocked does **not** prove the smoke detector triggered. This commits the logical error of **affirming the consequent**.\n\nJust because A causes B doesn't mean B only happens when A occurs. There could be other causes for the doors unlocking:\n- Manual override\n- Electronic malfunction\n- Scheduled maintenance unlock\n- Other building systems\n\n## Why the passage details don't change this\n\nThe passage actually emphasizes this point by providing alternative context:\n- The propped emergency exit (noon, unrelated to smoke detection)\n- Maintenance logs showing the unlock happened\n- Passing smoke detector inspection\n\nNone of this proves the smoke detector triggered. The passage demonstrates that events can occur on floor 3 without the smoke detector being involved—exactly why we cannot work backward from the effect to conclude the cause.",
