    "skill_name": "modus-ponens",
    "mode": "singlecall",
    "response": "# Answer: No\n\n## Analysis\n\nThis challenge tests whether we can validly apply modus ponens in reverse—a fallacy known as **affirming the consequent**.\n\n### Structure of the Argument\n\n**The Conditional (given):**\n- If [floor-3 smoke detector triggers] → then [stairwell doors on floors 2–4 unlock automatically]\n- Antecedent: floor-3 smoke detector triggers\n- Consequent: stairwell doors unlock\n\n**The Asserted Fact (given):**\n- The stairwell doors were unlocked at 2:14 p.m. yesterday\n\n### Why This Is Invalid\n\nWe have affirmed the **consequent** (the doors were unlocked), not the **antecedent** (the smoke detector triggered). The conditional tells us:\n\n*If smoke detector → then doors unlock*\n\nHowever, the mere fact that the doors unlocked does **not** prove the smoke detector was the cause. Multiple other mechanisms could unlock those doors:\n- Manual override by personnel\n- Electronic malfunction\n- Alternative automated triggers not mentioned in the protocol\n\n### Application of the Modus Ponens Constraints\n\nPer the skill guidelines: *\"Do not apply this pattern if only the consequent is asserted as true; that is a different and invalid inference.\"*\n\nThis passage does exactly that—it asserts only the consequent (unlocked doors) and omits any affirmation that the antecedent (detector trigger) actually occurred.\n\nThe passage even provides alternative explanations (propped emergency exit, untested maintenance status), reinforcing that the door unlock cannot be attributed solely to the smoke detector.\n\n**Conclusion: We cannot validly conclude that a floor-3 smoke detector triggered.**",
    "passed": true,
    "score": 1.0,
    "tokens": 1098,
    "elapsed_s": 8.02,
    "judge_rationale": "exact match: expected 'no', got 'no'"
  },
