    "response": "# Analysis: Modus Ponens Application\n\n## Identification of the Logical Structure\n\n**Conditional Statement (If-Then):**\n\"If a patron returns a book more than 14 days late, a $5 fee is added to their account.\"\n- Antecedent (the \"if\" part): A patron returns a book more than 14 days late\n- Consequent (the \"then\" part): A $5 fee is added to their account\n\n**Affirmed Antecedent:**\n\"Patron Delacroix returned 'The Cartographer's Daughter' 21 days past its due date last Friday.\"\n- This directly confirms the antecedent: Delacroix returned a book more than 14 days late (21 days > 14 days)\n\n## Valid Inference via Modus Ponens\n\nThe passage contains both required elements:\n1. ✓ A conditional statement establishing the rule\n2. ✓ An explicit affirmation that the antecedent is true (21 days late)\n\nFollowing the modus ponens form:\n- **If P (book returned >14 days late) then Q ($5 fee added)**\n- **P is true (21 days late)**\n- **Therefore, Q follows ($5 fee is added)**\n\n## Answer\n\n**A $5 fee is added to Delacroix's account.**\n\n## Why Other Information Does Not Affect This Conclusion\n\nThe passage mentions that \"fees are sometimes waived for first-time offenders\" and that no waiver has been recorded in Delacroix's file. However, this describes a *possible exception* to the policy, not an exception that has been *applied*. The absence of a recorded waiver indicates the standard policy applies. The fact that Delacroix also returned two other books on time is irrelevant to the fee calculation for the single late book.",
