    "response": "# Analysis: Should the Allergist Prescribe an Epinephrine Auto-Injector for Reyes?\n\n## Answer: Yes, per protocol.\n\n## Reasoning\n\n**The Protocol Requirement is Met:**\n\nThe clinic protocol establishes a clear, objective criterion: \"if a patient's IgE level for peanut protein exceeds 15 kU/L, the allergist prescribes an epinephrine auto-injector.\"\n\nPatient Reyes's test results satisfy this condition:\n- Her peanut IgE level: **22 kU/L**\n- Protocol threshold: **15 kU/L**\n- Status: **Well above the threshold** (as explicitly stated in the passage)\n\n## Addressing Potential Confusion\n\nThe passage includes information about anaphylaxis history to establish that this is *not* the only criterion for prescription:\n\n> \"Nurse Aabir notes that auto-injectors are also commonly prescribed for patients with documented anaphylaxis history.\"\n\nThe word \"also\" indicates anaphylaxis history is an *additional* reason for prescription, not a *prerequisite*. This is reinforced by noting Reyes has no anaphylaxis episodes—which would be irrelevant to mention if anaphylaxis history were required.\n\n## Clinical Logic\n\nThe IgE-based protocol likely exists because elevated IgE levels indicate immunologic sensitization and increased risk, independent of whether a patient has already experienced anaphylaxis. Waiting for a documented anaphylaxis episode before prescribing an auto-injector would be reactive rather than preventive.\n\n## Conclusion\n\nThe allergist should prescribe the epinephrine auto-injector for Patient Reyes based on her IgE level meeting the established protocol threshold.",
