    "mode": "singlecall",
    "response": "# Answer: Yes\n\n## Analysis\n\nThis argument exemplifies a **statistical syllogism**, which is a specific form of inductive inference with a particular logical structure.\n\n### Definition of Statistical Syllogism\n\nA statistical syllogism has this form:\n- Most/most likely members of group X have property P\n- Individual A is a member of group X\n- Therefore, A probably has property P\n\n### How the Argument Fits the Pattern\n\n**Major Premise:** Most regulars at Fernwood Cafe order espresso-based drinks during morning visits.\n\n**Minor Premise:** Tomas is a Fernwood Cafe regular.\n\n**Conclusion:** Tomas probably orders an espresso-based drink when he visits in the morning.\n\nThis directly matches the statistical syllogism structure.\n\n### Supporting Evidence from the Passage\n\nThe passage explicitly confirms this classification:\n- The analyst states conclusions are based on the documented regularity (3 years) and the statistical generalization (\"most regulars\")\n- The analyst **identifies this as an inductive inference** specifically, which is the broader category encompassing statistical syllogisms\n- The analyst acknowledges the non-deductive nature (\"probably orders,\" \"some regulars prefer\" alternatives), which is characteristic of statistical reasoning\n\n### Key Distinction\n\nWhile all statistical syllogisms are inductive inferences, not all inductive inferences are statistical syllogisms. This particular argument qualifies as a statistical syllogism because it moves from a statistical generalization about a group directly to a probabilistic conclusion about an individual member of that group.",
