    "response": "# Analysis: Is This a Statistical Syllogism?\n\n**Answer: Yes**\n\n## Justification\n\nThis argument satisfies all the required components of a statistical syllogism:\n\n**1. Majority Premise (Step 1)**\nThe passage explicitly establishes: \"most regulars at the Fernwood Cafe order an espresso-based drink during their morning visits.\" This uses the quantifier \"most,\" clearly indicating a generalization true for the majority (but not all) members of a defined group.\n\n**2. Group Membership Premise (Step 2)**\nThe passage states: \"Tomas has been documented as a Fernwood Cafe regular for the past three years.\" This asserts that Tomas is a member of the group referenced in the generalization.\n\n**3. Individual Conclusion (Step 3)**\nThe analyst concludes: \"Tomas probably orders an espresso-based drink when he visits in the morning.\" This applies the general trait to the specific individual.\n\n**4. Inductive (Not Deductive) Structure (Step 4)**\nThe argument is explicitly inductive: the analyst labels it as \"an inductive inference\" and acknowledges that \"some regulars prefer drip coffee or tea instead.\" The major premise allows exceptions and the conclusion uses the probabilistic qualifier \"probably,\" not certainty.\n\n**5. Matching Groups (Step 5)**\nAll three components refer to the same group: \"Fernwood Cafe regulars\" appears in the generalization, the membership claim, and implicitly in the conclusion's context.\n\nThe argument exemplifies the exact pattern described in the skill definition: moving from a statistical premise about most members of a group to a probabilistic conclusion about a particular member of that same group.",
