    "response": "# Analysis\n\n**Answer: No**\n\n## Reasoning\n\nThe orca argument is **not** a statistical syllogism, despite the student's claim that both arguments are the same kind of reasoning.\n\n### Application of the Skill Procedure\n\n**Step 1 & 4 – Identify the quantifier and inductive vs. deductive nature:**\nThe first premise states \"**All** mammals have lungs.\" This is a **universal quantifier**, not a statistical one (e.g., \"most,\" \"almost all,\" \"the majority\"). The argument claims an absolute, exception-free rule—making it **deductive**, not inductive/probabilistic.\n\n**Step 5 & 6 – Verify alignment of components:**\nWhile the orca argument does contain all three structural pieces (a general premise about a group, a membership claim, and a conclusion about an individual), the **type** of general premise disqualifies it from being a statistical syllogism.\n\n### Why the Student's Classification is Incorrect\n\nThe student conflates structural similarity with logical type. Both arguments share the form \"Group has property P; Individual X is in group; therefore X has P.\" However:\n\n- **Orca argument**: All mammals → universal premise → **categorical/deductive syllogism**\n- **Commuter argument**: Most commuters → statistical premise → **statistical syllogism**\n\nPer the constraint: \"Do not flag arguments whose major premise is universal ('all' or 'none') rather than statistical.\"\n\nThe conclusion's reference to \"a specific individual\" is insufficient grounds for classification; the nature of the major premise determines the syllogism type.",
