    "response": "**No.**\n\nThis passage is not an instance of statistical syllogism.\n\n**Reasoning:**\n\nAccording to the procedure for detecting statistical syllogism, three components must be present and aligned:\n\n1. **Majority premise** ✓ — Present: \"the majority of patrons who visit on Saturday mornings check out at least one cookbook\"\n\n2. **Membership premise** ✗ — Absent: There is no statement asserting that a specific individual is a member of the group (Saturday morning patrons)\n\n3. **Individual conclusion** ✗ — Absent: There is no conclusion drawn about any particular patron\n\nThe passage contains only the generalization itself. The librarian describes a statistical pattern about the group as a whole but does not apply this pattern to any named or specific person. As the passage explicitly states: \"She does not name any particular patron, draw any conclusion about a specific individual, or suggest that any named visitor will or has checked out a cookbook.\"\n\nA statistical syllogism requires movement from a group-level generalization to a conclusion about an individual case. This passage stops at the generalization stage. It would only become a statistical syllogism if, for example, the librarian had said: \"Therefore, when Maria comes in Saturday morning, she'll likely check out a cookbook\" — but no such individual application is made.",
