    "response": "# Answer: **No**\n\n## Analysis\n\nBased on the emotional-self-regulation skill framework, Captain Lindgren does **not** demonstrate emotional self-regulation in this passage.\n\n### Why This Fails the Criteria\n\n**Missing Emotional Trigger:**\nThe passage explicitly states that Lindgren \"rarely felt nervous\" and describes her remaining calm throughout the scenario. There is no evidence of an emotional state that requires modulation. She does not experience a triggering emotional shift—her composure is baseline and consistent.\n\n**Absence of Deliberate Modulating Action:**\nWhile the passage describes her calm behavior during turbulence (adjusting altitude, speaking in an even tone), these are professional *protocol-driven responses*, not deliberate self-directed emotional management. The text attributes her behavior to \"her training\" and what \"her training required,\" indicating external direction rather than internal self-regulation.\n\n**No State Management Demonstrated:**\nEmotional self-regulation requires evidence of *managing* an emotional state—initiating, inhibiting, or modulating it. The passage shows only the *output* of trained professional behavior, not an internal process of emotional control in response to an emotional stimulus.\n\n### Constraint Application\nPer the constraints: \"Do not flag routine calm behavior that is not preceded by an emotional trigger\" and \"Require both a triggering state and a deliberate modulating action; one alone is not sufficient.\"\n\nThis passage contains neither—only routine competent performance without preceding emotional disturbance.",
