    "response": "# Analysis: Direction of Marble Movement on a Tilted Tray\n\n## Answer\nThe marble moves **toward the opposite edge** (away from the cat).\n\n## Reasoning\n\nThis problem involves understanding gravity and the geometry of an inclined plane.\n\n### Key Physical Principles\n\n1. **Gravity acts downward**: The marble, like all objects, experiences gravitational force pulling it straight down toward Earth's center.\n\n2. **The tray is tilted**: When the cat presses down one edge, that edge becomes elevated relative to the opposite edge. The tray now forms an inclined plane.\n\n3. **The marble follows the path of least gravitational potential**: On an inclined surface, an object will roll or slide in the direction that takes it to lower elevation—that is, the direction the surface slopes downward.\n\n### Directional Analysis\n\n- **The cat's edge**: This is the high point of the incline (pressed down by the cat's weight, but from underneath the tray, making it an elevated edge relative to the surface)\n- **The opposite edge**: This is the low point of the incline, sloping downward from the cat's location\n\nThe passage states the tray \"slopes steadily from the cat's side toward the opposite side\"—this explicitly indicates a gradient running from the cat's position downward toward the far edge.\n\n### Why Other Factors Don't Apply\n\nThe passage specifically eliminates potential counterarguments:\n- \"No groove\" → the marble isn't constrained\n- \"Nothing blocking it\" → no barriers\n- \"Tray surface is dry and polished\" → minimal friction to prevent movement\n\n## Conclusion\n\nThe marble moves **toward the opposite edge** because gravity pulls it in the direction of the downward slope, which is away from the cat's elevated edge.",
