    "response": "# Analysis: Marble Motion on a Tilted Tray\n\n## Challenge Breakdown\n\nThe passage describes a scenario where:\n- A smooth, dry wooden tray is tilted by a cat pressing down one edge\n- A glass marble sits unobstructed on the tray's center\n- The tray maintains a steady slope for several seconds\n- The question asks: which direction does the marble move?\n\n## Application of Folk Physics\n\nThis challenge directly invokes everyday gravitational intuition about objects on inclined surfaces—a scenario virtually every adult has observed or experienced.\n\n**The intuitive physical principle:** An unsupported object on a frictionless (or very low-friction) tilted surface will move downhill due to gravity. It slides toward the lower side, not the higher side.\n\n**Applying this to the passage:**\n- The cat presses down on one edge, creating a slope\n- Gravity acts on the marble pulling it downward\n- The path of least resistance—and the direction gravity pulls—is toward the **lower edge** (the opposite side from the cat)\n- The smooth, dry, polished tray surface minimizes friction, so nothing substantially resists this gravitational pull\n\n## Answer\n\n**The marble moves toward the opposite edge** (away from the cat's side, toward the lower end of the tray).\n\n## Reasoning\n\nThis prediction requires no calculation, no specialized equipment, and no physics beyond everyday observation. Any adult who has rolled a ball down a ramp, watched water run downhill, or seen an object slide on a tilted surface would expect precisely this outcome. The marble follows gravity to the lower point—which is the side the cat is not pressing down.\n\nThe passage's emphasis on the tray being smooth, dry, and polished actually *strengthens* this prediction by removing friction as a complicating factor.",
