    "response": "# Analysis: Aiyana's Upper Body Motion Relative to the Bus\n\n## Answer: Aiyana's upper body lurches **forward** relative to the bus.\n\n## Explanation\n\nThis scenario involves understanding **inertia** and **reference frames**—specifically, the distinction between motion in the ground reference frame versus motion relative to the bus.\n\n### What Happens in the Ground Reference Frame\n\n1. **Before braking**: Aiyana and the bus both move forward at steady speed. There is no net force on Aiyana (Newton's First Law).\n\n2. **During braking**: \n   - The bus experiences a large backward force (friction from the road) that decelerates it\n   - Aiyana's feet experience friction from the bus floor that provides a backward force\n   - However, her upper body has **no direct force acting on it** to decelerate it\n\n3. **The critical point**: The friction between her feet and the floor is insufficient to decelerate her entire body at the same rate as the bus. Her lower body (feet and legs) decelerates with the bus due to friction, but her upper body continues forward at approximately the original speed due to inertia.\n\n### Relative to the Bus\n\nSince the bus is decelerating while Aiyana's upper body maintains its forward momentum longer, **relative to the bus**, her upper body appears to lurch forward. The bus floor is effectively moving backward beneath her, even though in absolute terms she's still moving forward—just slower than the bus.\n\n### Why Other Passengers Don't Lurch\n\nThe seated passengers are **belted in**, meaning the seatbelts provide the backward force needed to decelerate their entire bodies at the same rate as the bus. No such restraint exists for Aiyana's upper body.",
