    "response": "# Analysis: Water Flow from Tilted Kettle\n\n## Applying Folk Physics: Gravity and Liquid Behavior\n\nThis challenge hinges on a basic, everyday physical phenomenon that any untrained observer would predict: **how liquids respond to gravity and container tilt**.\n\n### The Intuitive Prediction\n\nWhen a container holding liquid is tilted, the liquid naturally flows downward due to gravity toward the lowest point of the container. An average person would expect:\n\n- **The water will flow out of the kettle's spout and into the cup.**\n\nThis is the universal, everyday outcome anyone would predict without hesitation when tilting a filled kettle over an empty cup positioned directly below.\n\n### Why This Reasoning Applies Here\n\nThe passage describes a straightforward scenario with no exceptions or complications:\n\n1. **Gravity is operating normally** – no mention of zero-gravity or unusual conditions\n2. **The setup is standard** – a tilted kettle with water reaching the spout opening, positioned directly over an open cup\n3. **The outcome is universally expected** – even a small child would predict water flows downward when a tilted container tips\n4. **No barriers block the flow** – the spout is open, the cup is directly below, nothing obstructs the path\n\n### Answer\n\n**The water will flow into the cup.**\n\nWhen Devon tilts the kettle until the water reaches the spout opening, gravity immediately pulls the water downward through the open spout directly into the cup positioned below. This is the outcome any untrained observer would confidently expect from this everyday scenario.",
