    "mode": "singlecall",
    "response": "# Analysis\n\nTo answer this challenge, I need to consider the physics of rolling objects down an inclined plane.\n\n**Key Physics Principles:**\n\nWhen objects roll (rather than slide) down a frictionless or low-friction ramp, their acceleration depends on their moment of inertia. For a solid sphere rolling without slipping, the acceleration down a ramp is:\n\na = (5/7)g sin(θ)\n\nThis formula is independent of:\n- The object's mass\n- The object's radius (size)\n- The object's material density\n\n**Application to the Two Balls:**\n\nBoth balls are:\n- The same size (same radius)\n- Released simultaneously with no initial push\n- Rolling freely down the same smooth ramp\n- Solid spheres (the rubber ball and steel bearing are both solid)\n\nSince the acceleration down the ramp depends only on gravity and the slope angle—not on mass, material, or size—both balls experience the same acceleration.\n\n**Conclusion:**\n\nThe rubber ball and steel ball bearing will reach the bottom at essentially the same time, despite their different materials and densities.\n\n---\n\n**Answer: They reach the bottom at roughly the same time.**",
    "passed": true,
    "score": 0.85,
    "tokens": 497,
    "elapsed_s": 3.37,
    "judge_rationale": "The response correctly concludes both balls arrive at roughly the same time and explains the physics. It addresses the common intuition implicitly via physics rather than explicitly naming the trap of assuming the steel ball wins, but the correct conclusion is clearly reached."
  },
