    "skill_name": "folk-physics-common-knowledge-physics",
    "mode": "singlecall",
    "response": "# Analysis\n\n## Applying Folk Physics to the Ramp Scenario\n\n**Scanning for everyday physical events:**\nThe passage describes two balls of identical size rolling down a smooth ramp under gravity, released simultaneously with no initial push.\n\n**Intuitive prediction an untrained observer would make:**\nMost people, without formal physics training, would expect that when two objects of the same size and shape are released together down the same smooth slope, they would arrive at the bottom at roughly the same time. The everyday intuition is: \"Same slope, same conditions, same release = same arrival.\"\n\n**Key physical factors within common perception:**\n- Both balls experience gravity equally (pulling them down the slope)\n- Both travel the same distance\n- The ramp is smooth (no significant friction difference expected for rubber vs. steel)\n- Neither ball is pushed or hindered\n\n**Where untrained intuition might falter:**\nAn untrained observer might initially think \"steel is heavier, so it should go faster\" or \"rubber might bounce/grip differently.\" However, the passage specifies both roll *freely* down a smooth slope. Common experience with objects sliding or rolling down smooth surfaces (playground slides, skateboard ramps) shows that object weight alone doesn't determine arrival time when friction is minimal and both objects follow the same path.\n\n**Critical insight from everyday observation:**\nA typical adult has likely observed that balls of similar size roll down inclines at comparable speeds regardless of whether they're made of rubber, plastic, or metal—the shape and smoothness of the surface matter far more than the material density.\n\n---\n\n## Answer\n\n**Roughly the same time.**",
