    "response": "# Analysis of Book Motion on Skateboard\n\n## The Physics Principle\n\nThis scenario illustrates **inertia and Newton's First Law of Motion**: an object in motion tends to stay in motion unless acted upon by an external force.\n\n## What Happens\n\n**Before collision:** The book rests on the skateboard, and both move together forward at the same velocity.\n\n**At collision:** The skateboard hits the threshold strip and **stops abruptly**. However, the book experiences no direct collision—there is no external force acting on it except friction from the skateboard's deck.\n\n**Critical point:** The friction between book and skateboard is insufficient to decelerate the book as rapidly as the skateboard decelerates. This is because:\n- Friction is a relatively weak force compared to the impact force stopping the skateboard\n- The book has inertia and tends to maintain its forward motion\n- There's nothing (no glue, tape, or barrier) preventing the book from sliding\n\n## Direction of Sliding\n\n**The book slides FORWARD** (in the original direction the skateboard was moving).\n\nFrom the skateboard's reference frame, the book appears to slide forward off the front of the skateboard's deck. From a stationary observer's perspective, the book continues moving forward due to inertia while the skateboard has stopped beneath it, causing relative forward motion.\n\n## Answer\n\nThe book slides **forward** (in the same direction as the skateboard's original motion), toward and off the front of the skateboard.",
