    "response": "# Analysis\n\n## Applying Folk Physics to the Challenge\n\nThis scenario involves **inertia and relative motion**—everyday physical concepts that untrained observers readily understand.\n\n### The Initial State\n- The skateboard and book are moving together in one direction (let's call it \"forward\").\n- Both have the same velocity and momentum.\n\n### The Critical Event\nWhen the skateboard's front wheels hit the threshold strip:\n- **The skateboard stops abruptly** (it decelerates rapidly due to the collision).\n- **The book is not attached or constrained** to the skateboard.\n\n### Intuitive Physical Prediction\n\nAn average person would immediately recognize this as a classic **inertia scenario**: an object in motion tends to continue in motion unless acted upon by a force. \n\nWhen the skateboard stops suddenly:\n- The skateboard experiences a large decelerating force (from the threshold).\n- The book experiences no such force (it's not glued or held).\n- The book's inertia causes it to **continue forward** in the original direction of motion.\n\nThe book does not \"know\" the skateboard has stopped; it slides forward *relative to the skateboard* due to its retained momentum.\n\n### Answer\n\n**The book slides forward** (in the skateboard's original direction of motion), off the front of the skateboard deck.\n\n---\n\n## Confidence in This Reasoning\n\nThis prediction requires only folk-physics understanding of:\n- Gravity (irrelevant here; the floor is level)\n- Inertia (universally understood: \"things keep moving unless stopped\")\n- Contact forces (the book has no contact force holding it back)\n\nThis is unambiguously the outcome any adult would expect without calculation or specialized knowledge.",
