    "response": "Yes.\n\nExplanation:\n\nThe CEO memo excerpt exhibits a clear example of the self-serving bias, where the individual takes personal credit for positive outcomes (revenue beat) and attributes negative consequences (margin compression due to increased costs and delayed production) to external factors beyond their control. This suggests that the CEO is trying to present himself in a favorable light, which is a hallmark of the self-serving bias.\n\nSpecific evidence from the passage includes:\n\n* The CEO takes personal credit for the revenue beat: \"a direct result of the pricing strategy I personally championed\"\n* The CEO attributes margin compression to external factors (tariffs and dock strike): \"but only because steel input costs rose 40%... and the dock strike delayed our highest-margin SKUs\"\n* The CEO uses phrases that highlight their own involvement or influence, such as \"I personally championed\" and \"our underlying execution was flawless\", which suggests a desire to take credit for successes and shift blame elsewhere.\n\nThese language patterns suggest that the self-serving bias is present in the CEO memo excerpt.",
