    "response": "Based on the passage, the answer is NO.\n\nHere's why:\n\n* The speaker, Dr. Okafor, attributes the success of Aim 1 (identifying the binding pocket) to both internal factors (Wei's exceptional sample prep) and external factors (the cryo-EM facility upgrade). This is not a pure self-serving attribution.\n* When attributing the failure of Aim 2, Dr. Okafor takes full responsibility by saying \"That was my mistake\" and implies that it was an internal error on his part.\n\nTo satisfy the criteria for self-serving bias, we would need to see a pattern where favorable outcomes (success) are paired with internal causes AND unfavorable outcomes (failure) are paired with external causes. However, in this passage, Dr. Okafor's attribution of failure is internally attributed (\"my mistake\"), which does not demonstrate the asymmetric causal attribution characteristic of self-serving bias.\n\nTherefore, I conclude that the self-serving bias pattern is NOT present in Dr. Okafor's report.",
