    "mode": "singlecall",
    "response": "After analyzing the passage, I conclude that Manager B (Owen) exhibits the self-serving bias pattern.\n\nThe self-serving bias is a cognitive error where individuals attribute their successes to internal, controllable factors and their failures to external, uncontrollable factors (Berg et al., 2000). In Owen's response, he:\n\n1. Attributes Q3 success to an uncontrollable factor: \"the new CRM rollout — which IT, not I, drove\" (emphasis mine). By shifting the credit to another department, Owen minimizes his own contribution.\n2. Attributes Q4 failure to a controllable factor: \"I prioritized the wrong accounts and didn't coach the junior reps enough.\" This implies that Owen could have done better if he had made different decisions.\n\nIn contrast, Manager A (Tess) attributes both successes and failures to factors that she can control:\n\n* She takes credit for Q3 success by restructuring the workflow, which is an internal decision.\n* She blames Q4 failure on external, uncontrollable factors: procurement's mistakes with vendor contracts and HR's inability to fill open roles.\n\nThis distinction demonstrates that Owen exhibits the self-serving bias pattern by attributing his successes to others and blaming his failures on himself.",
