    "response": "Based on the passage, Manager A (Tess) exhibits the self-serving bias pattern.\n\nHere's why:\n\n1. **Favorable outcome attributed internally**: Tess attributes their team's successful Q3 to her own restructuring of the workflow (\"I restructured the workflow\").\n2. **Unfavorable outcome attributed externally**: However, when explaining the missed Q4 targets, Tess shifts blame to external factors: \"procurement keeps fumbling vendor contracts\" and \"HR can't fill the two open roles I requested in July.\"\n3. **Asymmetrical attribution**: Tess attributes success to her own actions but blames others for failure.\n4. **Self-esteem protection or enhancement**: By deflecting responsibility for Q4's failure onto procurement and HR, Tess protects her self-image as a capable manager.\n\nIn contrast, Manager B (Owen) also takes responsibility for both the favorable outcome in Q3 (\"I prioritized the wrong accounts\") and the unfavorable outcome in Q4 (\"didn't coach the junior reps enough\"). Owen attributes both outcomes to his own actions, which does not demonstrate the self-serving bias pattern.",
