    "response": "**No**\n\nHere's why:\n\nAccording to the passage, the Skill of Accidental Fallacy (also known as Fallacy of Accident) occurs when an informal argument applies a general rule to a specific case while overlooking a legitimate exception to that rule.\n\nIn this passage, the major premise \"Every bachelor is an unmarried man\" makes no exceptions. It's not stating that all bachelors are always unmarried men unless it's explicitly stated that Tomas falls under some sort of unstated exception, which the passage does not do.\n\nThe student claims that Tomas might fall under an unstated exception. However, the evidence from the passage actually shows that Tomas fits within this generalization without any recognized exceptions: Tomas is indeed a bachelor according to public records and his own statement, so it's safe to apply the rule-of-thumb.\n\nTherefore, there is no accident-fallacy here because Tomas does not possess qualities which would exclude him from being an unmarried man.",
