    "model": "llama3.1:8b",
    "condition": "curated",
    "skill_name": "statistical-syllogism",
    "mode": "singlecall",
    "response": "**Yes**\n\nHere's why:\n\n1. The passage contains a generalization (\"most regulars at the Fernwood Cafe order an espresso-based drink during their morning visits\") that is true for the most part, using the quantifier \"most\" to indicate a majority.\n2. It states that Tomas has been documented as a regular at the Fernwood Cafe for the past three years, indicating his membership in this group.\n3. The analyst concludes that Tomas probably orders an espresso-based drink when he visits in the morning, applying the general trait (ordering an espresso-based drink) to the specific member (Tomas).\n4. The inference is probabilistic rather than deductive, as noted by the analyst: \"this an inductive inference\", and it explicitly states that some regulars prefer alternative options, allowing for exceptions.\n5. All three components align: the group (\"regulars at the Fernwood Cafe\") matches between the generalization and Tomas's membership claim, and the trait attributed to the individual (ordering an espresso-based drink) is the same as in the generalization.\n\nTherefore, this argument meets all the criteria of a statistical syllogism.",
    "passed": false,
    "score": 0.0,
    "tokens": 861,
    "elapsed_s": 4.16,
    "judge_rationale": "no match: expected 'yes', got 'therefore, this argument meets all the criteria of a statistical syllogism'"
