    "skill_name": "emotional-self-regulation",
    "mode": "singlecall",
    "response": "Yes.\n\nExplanation:\n\nThe passage provides evidence of a specific emotional trigger (mild turbulence) that necessitates a modulation of the captain's state. Despite having flown for twenty years and being rarely nervous, when the turbulence begins, she adjusts altitude calmly as per protocol, indicating deliberate action taken to modulate her reaction. Moreover, informing passengers in an even tone also shows self-directed effort to maintain composure during the challenging situation.\n\nAll criteria outlined for emotional self-regulation are met:\n\n1. A character (Captain Lindgren) experiencing a shift from her typical state due to a triggering stimulus (turbulence).\n2. An explicit action taken to initiate, inhibit, or modulate that state: adjusting altitude calmly per protocol and speaking in an even tone.\n3. The action is self-directed and aims at managing one's own emotion or behavior.\n4. A specific emotional stimulus triggers the modulation of her state (turbulence).\n5. Her actions align with explicit protocol and training requirements, ensuring that the emotional regulation is deliberate and aimed at maintaining safety standards rather than just surface-level calmness.\n\nTherefore, Captain Lindgren engages in emotional self-regulation as depicted in the passage.",
