    "response": "To determine whether Aiyana's upper body will lurch forward or backward relative to the bus, we need to consider the forces acting on her body during the sudden deceleration of the bus.\n\nAccording to Newton's second law of motion, when a force is applied to an object, it changes its momentum. In this case, the force of friction between Aiyana's shoes and the floor keeps her feet in place, but her upper body is unrestrained. As the bus decelerates sharply, Aiyana will experience an inertial reaction force due to the conservation of momentum.\n\nSince the delivery truck ahead has suddenly stopped, the bus behind it must slow down rapidly to avoid a collision. This rapid deceleration means that the passengers inside the bus are initially accelerating forward, trying to maintain their original velocity. As they suddenly realize they need to stop, their upper bodies will continue to move in this initial direction for a brief moment before the deceleration of the bus causes them to slow down.\n\nHowever, Aiyana's feet being restrained by friction does not directly influence her upper body. What really matters here is how her entire body responds to the deceleration. In an inertial reference frame attached to the bus (i.e., relative to other passengers on the bus), every part of her body will maintain its initial velocity and then slow down together due to the shared momentum. Therefore, since Aiyana's upper body has no external forces acting upon it, such as a seatbelt or being physically restrained by something in front of her (like a pole would have done in a different scenario), and she is essentially free to move within the bus with the rest of her lower body already slowed down due to friction, her entire body will not be able to decelerate more quickly than her upper part.\n\nThus, Aiyana's upper body lurches forward relative to the bus during the sudden stop. This is because the forces acting on her body are such that the part of her body she can