eing restrained like those seated passengers who are belted in, will experience this transition from continued movement to deceleration more significantly.\n5. **Confirm the physical claim involved is one an average person would accept without instruments or calculation**: Yes, the behavior of objects under inertia and the action of friction are intuitive concepts that any adult could understand without needing special knowledge.\n\nGiven these considerations:\n\n- When a bus brakes sharply after stopping ahead, it is common to feel pushed back into your seat (inertia), but in this scenario, Aiyana's upper body would continue moving due to its initial velocity relative to the rest of the bus for that split second before coming to rest.\n- Therefore, based on our analysis and intuitive understanding of physics at play here, it is logical to conclude that during this sharp deceleration phase, Aiyana’s upper body will lurch **forward**.\n\nThis conclusion is drawn from understanding inertia's influence in everyday situations.",
