    "response": "Based on the principles of Newton's laws of motion, specifically the law of inertia and conservation of momentum, we can analyze the situation.\n\nAs the skateboard rolls smoothly across the floor, it maintains its original velocity until an external force acts upon it. In this case, when the front wheels hit the raised threshold strip, the skateboard abruptly stops due to frictional forces opposing its motion.\n\nHowever, since the book is not attached or in contact with the skateboard at the time of impact, it will continue moving forward as if it were on a separate entity. This is because the book's momentum is not directly affected by the deceleration of the skateboard.\n\nAs the book continues to move forward due to its retained momentum, it will encounter frictional forces opposing its motion on the surface of the deck. These frictional forces act in the direction opposite to the motion of the book.\n\nSince the frictional forces are acting on the book, and the book is not glued or held in place, it will slide off the skateboard in the direction of the external force applied by these frictional forces. This means that relative to the skateboard's original direction of motion, the book will slide **backward**.\n\nThe key evidence from the passage supporting this analysis is: \"The front wheels of the skateboard hit a small raised threshold strip across the floor, and the skateboard stops abruptly.\" This indicates that the external force acting on the skateboard is due to frictional forces opposing its motion. Since the book's momentum is conserved, it will continue moving in the original direction until these frictional forces act upon it, causing it to slide backward.\n\nTherefore, based on this analysis, we can conclude that the book will slide off the skateboard **backward** relative to the original direction of motion.",
