    "response": "To answer this challenge, we need to apply our understanding of common physical phenomena as a typical untrained observer would expect.\n\n## Step 1: Assess the initial condition\nMarco sets the hot skillet directly onto the granite countertop without any insulation. We know the skillet is hot enough to make oil sputter just moments before, which indicates it has retained significant heat from being on the burner.\n\n## Step 2: Consider the heat transfer\nHeat transfer occurs through conduction and radiation. When the hot skillet comes into contact with the cooler countertop, some of its heat will be transferred to the countertop through conduction. However, this process is slow compared to other forms of heat transfer like convection, which isn't applicable in this scenario since the kitchen is undisturbed.\n\n## Step 3: Analyze the effect of time\nOver fifteen minutes, more significant heat can be transferred from the skillet to the countertop due to prolonged contact. The granite countertop at normal room temperature provides a path for thermal energy to dissipate out of the skillet.\n\n## Step 4: Determine the expected change in temperature\nGiven that heat has been continuously dissipated into the cooler environment and the countertop, we can expect the skillet's temperature to decrease over time. Thus, the skillet would be cooler when Marco returns compared to when he set it down initially.\n\nThe final answer is: **cooler**",
