    "mode": "singlecall",
    "response": "**Yes**\n\n### Analysis of the Passage\n\n**Definition of Self-Serving Bias**\nSelf-serving bias is a cognitive distortion characterized by the tendency to attribute positive outcomes to one's own abilities or characteristics (internal attribution) while attributing negative outcomes to external factors (external attribution). It serves as a psychological mechanism for preserving a positive self-image.\n\n**Evidence from the Passage**\nThe founder explicitly constructs the narrative using this specific dichotomy:\n\n1.  **Attribution of Positive Outcomes (Internal):**\n    The passage states that viral growth was achieved \"because of the brilliant onboarding flow my team and I designed from scratch\" and \"We knew exactly what users needed.\" By highlighting the *quality* of their design and the *talent* of the team (\"my team and I\"), the founder attributes the success to her own leadership and capabilities.\n\n2.  **Attribution of Negative Outcomes (External):**\n    When discussing the recent slowdown, the founder lists the following external causes without any criticism of her own judgment or strategy:\n    *   \"the entire ad market collapsed\"\n    *   \"Apple changed its tracking rules\"\n    *   \"a competitor got bought by Google\"\n    She adds the caveat that \"no one in our space could have grown through that,\" directly isolating these events as unforeseeable and unavoidable external forces.\n\n**Conclusion**\nThere is no evidence in the text suggesting she attributed the growth to something other than her own team's design or knowledge. The entire framing of the event is centered on distinguishing between controllable factors (the design process) and uncontrollable factors (market shifts), which is the hallmark of self-serving bias.\n\nAnswer: **Yes**",
