    "response": "**Yes**\n\nThe self-serving bias pattern is clearly present in the quote. The narrator admits to specific, tangible failures in their writing process (\"rushed the second act,\" \"dialogue was wooden,\" \"ignored my editor's structural notes\") to explain the critical failure of their debut. However, upon facing the consequence (a \"bombed\" debut), they attribute the failure to an external, externalizing factor: their own lack of readiness (\"I wasn't ready as a craftsman\").\n\nThis pattern functions as a cognitive bias where individuals deflect external or self-inflicted failures (often related to effort or skill) onto an external agent (like a boss, society, or the situation itself). In this case, Aaron attributes the success of the new book not to genuine improvement or hard work in the face of adversity, but to a sudden, supernatural realization that they finally learned how to revise, while simultaneously blaming their past failure of rushing. He separates the two outcomes but uses a similar self-serving logic to justify each: the new book's success is his \"outreach,\" and his past failure was just \"not being ready\" rather than the result of poor craft.",
