    "response": "**Yes**\n\nBased on Jamal's own attributions within the passage, the self-serving bias pattern is present.\n\nSelf-serving bias is defined as a cognitive tendency where individuals attribute positive outcomes to their own characteristics or efforts while attributing negative outcomes to external factors or bad luck.\n\nAnalyzing Jamal's specific statements reveals this pattern clearly:\n1.  **Positive Outcome (Academics):** Jamal states, \"I aced chem **because I memorized every reaction mechanism**.\" Here, Jamal attributes his success entirely to his own specific, controllable effort (memorizing mechanisms). This is a classic example of the self-serving element.\n2.  **Negative Outcome (Disappointment):** Jamal states, \"I tanked the history exam **because the professor writes unfair trick questions**...\" In this context, Jamal attributes his failure to an external, uncontrollable factor (the professor's grading criteria) rather than acknowledging factors within his own control (e.g., lack of preparation).\n\nSince Jamal attributes success to his own ability/effort and failure to an external cause, the structure of his reasoning aligns perfectly with the definition of the self-serving bias.\n\n**Answer:** Yes",
