    "response": "The metaphor **\"The prosecution's case is a house of cards\"** uses the imagery of an unstable structure to illustrate the fragility and eventual collapse of the legal evidence presented. In this context, the **tenor** (the literal object of the comparison) is the **evidence or case brought by the prosecution**.\n\nThe **vehicle** (the comparison) is the structure of a **house of cards**, a metaphor for anything built on the tips of thin, easily displaced objects that collapses when a single wind gust blows.\n\nBy equating the prosecution's case to a house of cards, the speaker argues that the case is constructed on very weak and fragile evidence. Any minor flaw, contradiction, or unconfirmed detail could cause the entire structure to crumble, causing the prosecution's claim of guilt to fall apart before it even reaches the jury. This specific use of the metaphor serves as a critique of the strength of the prosecution's legal argument.",
