    "response": "Based on the passage provided, here is the specific action that shows Dr. Patel regulating her own emotional state, supported by evidence within the text:\n\n**The Action:**\nThe specific action is Dr. Patel **closing her eyes and exhaling slowly four times** while **closing her eyes**.\n\n**Evidence from the Passage:**\nThe text describes this sequence of actions exactly as follows:\n> \"Halfway down the corridor, she stopped at a window, **closed her eyes**, and **exhaled slowly four times until her shoulders dropped**. Then she straightened her coat and continued.\"\n\n**Analysis:**\nThis action demonstrates emotional regulation through a deliberate physiological technique:\n1.  **Sensory Regulation (Closing Eyes):** By closing her eyes, she creates a break from external stimuli (the waiting family and the hospital environment) and focuses her internal attention on herself.\n2.  **Physiological Release (Exhaling):** The act of exhaling slows the heart rate and lowers blood pressure. In the context of the story, this action immediately causes her shoulders to drop (\"until her shoulders dropped\"), signaling a shift from a tense, panicked state to a calmer, more controlled one.\n3.  **Cessation of Regurgitation:** By ending the emotional outburst and exhaling before speaking, she physically separates the raw panic from the delivery of the news, allowing her voice to remain \"steady\" as described later in the sentence.\n\nTherefore, closing her eyes and performing four slow exhalations is the specific regulatory mechanism she employs to de-escalate her immediate emotional state.",
