s moving forward at the bus's original speed.\n\nFrom the perspective of someone sitting in a different seat or standing still on a sidewalk:\n*   The bus moves backward.\n*   If Aiyana is standing on the floor of the moving bus, she moves *forward* in the direction of the bus's motion.\n\nTherefore, relative to the bus itself, her upper body will lurch **forward**.\n\n### **Conclusion**\n\nThe upper body lurches **forward**.\n\nThis outcome relies entirely on the intuitive understanding of inertia (Newton's First Law) applied to daily life: an object in motion stays in motion, and it is only the part in contact with the ground (feet/shoes) that experiences the sudden deceleration of the vehicle. Without the restraint of a seatbelt, the upper body cannot follow the bus to a stop.",
