    "response": "Yes, the self-serving bias pattern is present in this passage.\n\nThe self-serving bias occurs when an individual attributes positive outcomes to themselves while attributing negative outcomes to others or external factors. In the passage, Dr. Patel demonstrates this pattern through the following reasoning:\n1.  **Attribution of Success:** She explicitly identifies her personal success: \"My survival rate on the Whipple procedures was the highest in the department.\" This suggests she is viewing her own achievement as a reflection of her personal capabilities rather than an objective group performance.\n2.  **Attribution of Failure:** She immediately counters the negatives with personal explanations: \"The three post-op infections... were unfortunate, but the hospital's HVAC system was under repair...\" by pointing to factors *outside* her control, rather than her own conduct.\n3.  **Conclusion:** She concludes that \"I\" is the cause of the success (survival rate being highest) and that the negative events (infections) were caused by an external factor (the HVAC issue).\n\nThis distinction is crucial: attributing success only to \"self\" contradicts standard self-serving bias, while attributing failures to others or specific external circumstances aligns with the self-serving bias pattern. Therefore, the self-serving bias is the dominant or most prevalent pattern described.",
