    "response": "Based on the passage provided, the answer is **no**. The self-serving bias pattern does not appear in the text.\n\nThe passage illustrates a **competitive, non-biased perspective**, which is the opposite of self-serving bias. Here is the analysis supporting this:\n\n1.  **Objective Attribution of Success**: Coach Ruiz attributes his winning to his own preparation (\"drilled the bunt-defense rotation\") and the players' execution of his system. In this view, the results are a result of skill and strategy rather than external factors like luck, timing, or team composition.\n2.  **Attribution of Failure**: Conversely, when Game Two fails, Ruiz focuses entirely on the team's performance rather than an objective assessment of their actual ability. The loss is explained as a result of unfavorable conditions (\"umpire's strike zone was a joke,\" \"field was waterlogged,\" \"scheduler stuck us with a 30-minute turnaround\") which are described as external barriers to success.\n3.  **Absence of Self-Involvement**: The narrative does not show Coach Ruiz blaming himself for the lack of success. He remains focused solely on his own performance and the team's execution.\n\nTherefore, the content of the passage highlights the **achievement of the team's goals**, not a bias of the coach toward himself or a negative view of his team.",
