ould have height = 80.\n    - However, is there a contradiction in the passage that forces the long side to be small?\n    - If the painting was \"too wide to fit through a 32-inch door\" when placed vertically, that confirms the short side (36\") > 32\".\n    - But does that tell us anything about the long side? Not necessarily. A 36\" by 80\" rectangle also has a long side > 80\".\n    - Let's look at the phrasing again: \"It would not fit... in that orientation.\" This means the object *didn't* fit in that specific orientation.\n    - Is it possible the passage implies the long side is actually *smaller*? No, 36\" (short) vs 32\" (door width). A short side can't be 36\" if the door was 32\" wide, because width > height would mean 36 > 32, which works. But if the painting's long side was, say, 72 inches and its short side was 24 inches, then the short side (24\") > 32\" is false. So the painting would fit in that orientation. The text says it **would not fit**. This confirms the painting's short side was not greater than the door width (32\"). So Short Side > 32\".\n    - So far: Painting's short side > 32\".\n    - We are asked: Is the long side > 80\"?\n    - If the painting is a rectangle, Long Side > Short Side.\n    - We know Short Side > 32\".\n    - If the Long Side were > 80\", then 72 (long side 72) > 80 (door height) would require a contradiction (72 < 80).\n    - Wait, if the painting is 72 inches long, can it fit through a 80-inch door?\n    - \"The painting's width exceeded the door's width.\" This refers to the short side fitting into the short side.\n    - It does **not** say anything about the short side fitting through the tall part of the door (the 80 inches).\n    - However, let's re-read the prompt's \"Challenge\" carefully.\n    - Challenge: \"Is the painting's long side greater than 80 inches?\"\n    - Evidence from passage:\n        1. Painting dimensions: 36\" short side. Implies long side = 72\" (if it's a rectangle).\n        2. Doorway d