    "response": "Based on the details provided in the passage, we can deduce the direction of Aiyana's movement relative to the bus through an analysis of her balance and the forces acting upon her.\n\nFirst, let's establish the baseline state of the situation. Aiyana is in the aisle of a bus, standing upright. Crucially, she is **not holding a pole** and is **not belted**. This means she relies entirely on her own balance and her feet on the floor to stay centered in her seat.\n\nWhen the bus driver slams the brakes hard, the bus experiences a rapid deceleration. In the frame of reference of the bus, an object moving forward appears to slow down or stop abruptly. For Aiyana to stay balanced on her feet in this position, her body weight must not be directed away from her feet relative to the bus's forward path.\n\nIf Aiyana's upper body were to lurch forward (relative to the bus), it would imply that her center of gravity is shifting forward or her mass is shifting away from her feet. If her upper body moves forward while her feet remain planted, she would simply slide forward off the floor. However, the passage states that **nothing else restrains her body**, implying she cannot slide off.\n\nTherefore, the only way to remain centered when both feet are stationary relative to the bus and no external restraint (like a pole or belts) is for her center of mass to stay relative to the bus's frame. If the bus accelerates backward, she would lurch forward; if it stops, she must remain still or accelerate backward at the same rate as the bus. But since she is not holding a pole, the only force keeping her stationary relative to the bus is the normal force of the floor. Thus, her body must stay aligned with the bus's forward direction.\n\n**Conclusion:**\n\nRelative to the bus, Aiyana's upper body **lurches forward**.",
