    "response": "Based on the passage provided, the answer can be deduced by analyzing the physical properties of the object and the immediate environment described.\n\n**1. Analysis of the Helium Balloon**\nThe text explicitly states that the balloon is \"helium-filled.\" In standard physics and general knowledge, helium gas is less dense than air. When released from a balloon, it rises to a greater height than the surrounding air and eventually ascends until it reaches an equilibrium point known as the \"critical altitude.\" At this height, the buoyant force of the air becomes sufficient to counteract the gravitational force of the helium. At this specific location, the balloon is buoyed up by the ambient air and becomes stationary relative to the ceiling, meaning it **stops rising and remains aloft** at that point.\n\n**2. Analysis of the Environment**\nThe passage describes a setting with \"a tall ceiling,\" \"no fans,\" and \"no open windows.\" Crucially, there is no ventilation system blowing air near the room, and the room appears calm. This implies a relatively stable atmospheric pressure, although the balloon itself was \"unweighted at the bottom.\"\n\n**3. Analyzing the Release Action**\nThe text specifies that Mira \"let go of the ribbon entirely\" because she was distracted by the cake. She did not attempt to retrieve it. Since the helium balloon is the object in question, and it was released from the balloon, the action of releasing the helium implies that it has just been released from the balloon's interior.\n\n**Conclusion**\nThe helium balloon starts on the ceiling and rises, reaching its critical altitude where it stops ascending. At that exact point, the balloon is buoyed up by the air and rests on the ceiling, effectively coming to a rest before rising again. Therefore, the released helium balloon ends up resting on the **ceiling of the room**, as it rises to a height where it becomes buoyed and stationary before ascending again.",
